# --- What the transcription actually did ------------------------------------
#
# Three findings, all measured on these four clips, all recorded in
# settle/text/voice.py and SPEC A134 rather than quietly fixed.
#
# 1. It returned URDU. `language="hi"`, no prompt, and all four clips came back
#    in Arabic script. DECISIONS warned about Devanagari; the reality was a
#    third script no parser was written for, and every clip would have
#    classified as unclear.
#
# 2. It TRUNCATED. Clip 1's un-prompted transcript stops at "agle mahine kar
#    dunga" and drops the self-correction that is the clip's only purpose. The
#    audio contains it. Confirmed by re-transcribing the same file with a prompt
#    and getting the clause back.
#
# 3. It was NOT DETERMINISTIC. Four calls on clip 1 at the default temperature
#    gave three different strings, one truncated. At temperature 0, 4/4
#    identical.
#
# A romanised-Hinglish `prompt` fixes 1 and 2; `temperature=0` fixes 3. Both are
# correctness settings. PROMPT_VERSION is part of the cache key so a steering
# change invalidates the transcripts it produced.
#
# The clips themselves are fine. Nothing here is a recording problem.

# --- out/llm_cache.json is NOT committed, and does not need to be -------------
#
# It is a run artefact, gitignored, and not on this allowlist. The first attempt
# at this checkpoint force-added it, which the gate correctly refused.
#
# The prompt says to commit "the clips, the transcripts, and out/voice_demo.json"
# — and the transcripts are already in out/voice_demo.json, which is allowlisted
# and carries every clip's transcript beside the sha256 of the audio that
# produced it. So `transcribe()` reads three sources in order: the run cache,
# then that committed artefact, then the model.
#
# The result is better than force-adding the cache would have been. A fresh
# clone has no cache and no key and still replays every clip, and
# `test_VOI_1_a_fresh_clone_replays_with_no_cache_and_no_key` asserts exactly
# that state rather than assuming it — it points CACHE_PATH at an empty file,
# deletes OPENAI_API_KEY, and requires every clip to resolve with source=DEMO.
#
# The artefact is keyed on the same content hash as the cache, so the two cannot
# disagree about which audio a transcript belongs to.

# --- python-multipart, and why the route takes a raw body --------------------
#
# FastAPI's `UploadFile` needs `python-multipart`, which is not pinned, and
# `requirements.txt` is not on this allowlist. Installing it into .venv without
# declaring it would make the endpoint work here and fail for anyone cloning the
# repo — the trade CP12 refused over `httpx`, for the same reason.
#
# So `POST /voice/extract` reads the audio from the request body and takes the
# filename from a header. The webhook already reads raw bytes for its own
# reasons, so the shape is not new. If a future checkpoint opens
# requirements.txt, `python-multipart` plus `UploadFile` is the tidier form and
# nothing else about the route changes.
